      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Results of previous studies with stents coated with 'biocompatible' polymers showed that severe inflammatory reaction and subsequent in-stent restenosis may occur."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To evaluate the contribution of granulomatous reaction from uncoated stents to formation of in-stent neointimal hyperplasia."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Uncoated stainless-steel stents were implanted into 21 porcine coronary arteries without oversizing and harvested after 2 months (n = 6) or 6 months (n = 7). We compared the stents with granulomatous reaction with those without foreign-body reaction."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Granulomatous reactions occurred in five 21 stents and resulted in there being significantly greater in-stent neointimal hyperplasia than there was with stents without foreign-body reaction (angiographic diameter stenosis 45 +/- 36 versus 16 +/- 16%, area of neointimal 3.30 +/- 1.4 versus 1.22 +/- 0.4 mm2, thickness of neointima 0.46 +/- 0.29 versus 0.11 +/- 0.09 mm, stenosed area 56 +/- 24 versus 20 +/- 7%, P < 0.01 for each comparison). This increase in amount of neointima was accompanied by significantly greater proliferating cell nuclear antibody staining (15 +/- 5 versus 3 +/- 2%, P < 0.05) in the presence of a granuloma near the stent struts."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "A localized granulomatous reaction is associated with a significant increase in amount of stent neointima and proliferation of cells. Thus, permanent stent implants may provoke granulomatous vascular reactions that may affect late-healing responses and clinical outcomes."
        }
      ]},
